<Project>

  <!--
    Copyright (c) 2026 Lars Brubaker, MatterHackers Inc.

    The browser leg of PolygonMesh's CSG backend: whether the wasm build of manifold_rs is linked
    into a head's wasm module. The wgpu twin of this is WebGpu/build/WebGpuBrowser.targets, and the
    two are deliberately the same shape.

    Why a head has to say anything at all. On desktop the ManifoldRust package's natives are probed
    out of runtimes/<rid>/native at runtime and nobody decides anything. A browser has no dynamic
    loading, so the archive has to be on mono-wasm's emcc link line, and the package's own
    build/ManifoldRust.props puts it there for ANY browser-wasm consumer - it defaults
    ManifoldRustLinkBrowserWasm to true off the RuntimeIdentifier alone. That default is right for an
    application that does booleans and wrong for everything else, because "consumer of the package"
    is a much wider set than "does CSG": PlatformBrowser reaches PolygonMesh through RenderGl, so
    every agg browser head is one, whether or not it has ever called a boolean. Without this file
    examples/BrowserHost - which exists to prove the host boots and paints - silently grew a 5.3 MB
    archive and a full emcc relink, and its README's promise that a plain publish needs no wasm-tools
    workload stopped being true.

    So: off by default, and the head's own switch maps onto it. -p:LinkManifoldRust=true turns it
    back on, deliberately the same spelling and the same semantics as LinkEmdawnWebGpu next door.
    Without it the manifold_rs DllImports are unbacked and the first CSG call throws - the right
    failure for a build that never asked for the native link. A global -p:ManifoldRustLinkBrowserWasm=true
    still works as a direct escape hatch, because a global property outranks the assignment below.

    Import it from the head's csproj, for the same reason WebGpuBrowser.targets is imported there
    rather than flowed along a ProjectReference: a .targets that a reference brings with it cannot
    set a property in the consuming project's evaluation, and the consuming project is where the
    package's props reads it. Importing it from something that is not a browser head costs nothing -
    the package's own condition is on the RuntimeIdentifier, so all of this is inert off wasm.

    One thing this file deliberately does NOT do: point WasmCachePath at a writable Emscripten
    cache. A head that links manifold and ALSO links emdawnwebgpu gets that from
    WebGpuBrowser.targets. A head that links manifold alone has to do it itself - the workload's own
    cache is used frozen, for the reasons written up over there - and MatterCAD.Wasm is the one head
    that does. Reaching WebGpu's property from PolygonMesh's build asset would couple the two
    libraries for one caller's benefit.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Assigned rather than defaulted-when-empty, which would never fire: NuGet's generated imports
      come in near the top of Microsoft.Common.props, so the package's props has already run by the
      time a csproj body imports this and has already set the property to true. Conditioning on
      LinkManifoldRust alone rather than on the current value also makes this correct whichever
      order the two files happen to evaluate in.
    -->
    <ManifoldRustLinkBrowserWasm Condition="'$(LinkManifoldRust)' != 'true'">false</ManifoldRustLinkBrowserWasm>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Keeps the wasm archive itself out of the publish output, whether or not it was linked.

    The package carries it at runtimes/browser-wasm/native/manifold_rs.a, and for a browser-wasm RID
    the SDK treats everything under runtimes/<rid>/native as a runtime asset and publishes it - which
    is right for a desktop .dylib the runtime will dlopen and wrong for this. A wasm .a is a LINK
    input: either it is already inside dotnet.native.wasm or it is not wanted at all, and nothing at
    runtime ever probes for it. Left alone it lands as 5.3 MB beside the app in every browser head's
    publish folder - not served, since it is outside wwwroot, but a misleading artifact in the
    deployable output. 0.4.2 had no browser-wasm leg and so shipped nothing here; this keeps that true.

    Unconditional on purpose: the linked case wants it gone just as much as the unlinked one. Removing
    it here cannot affect the link, which reads @(NativeFileReference) straight out of the package
    cache and has long since run.

    Same hook and same shape as _DropDesktopWgpuNativeFromPublish in WebGpu/build/WebGpuBrowser.targets,
    which solves the mirror-image problem for the desktop wgpu-native library.
  -->
  <Target Name="_DropManifoldRustArchiveFromPublish" AfterTargets="ComputeFilesToPublish">
    <ItemGroup>
      <ResolvedFileToPublish Remove="@(ResolvedFileToPublish)"
                             Condition="'%(Filename)' == 'manifold_rs' AND '%(Extension)' == '.a'" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
